WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.220
We are absolutely destroying these horrible
regulations.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.440
I haven’t seen anything like the regulatory
reductions that have taken place.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.180
Let’s cut the red tape.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.980
Who is in favor of regulations that aren't
working?

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.200
We have undertaken a historic effort to massively
reduce job-crushing regulations.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.560
And we’re imposing a new rule which mandates
that for every one new regulation,

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.020
two old regulations must be eliminated.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.920
Donald Trump promised at the beginning of
his presidency to slash regulations.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.679
And after his first year, he’s delivered
more than almost anybody predicted.

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:47.010
Washington’s regulatory reformers have infiltrated
the halls of power

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.940
and are attempting to engineer the most ambitious
pruning of the executive branch

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.300
in over three decades.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.769
It’s a bright spot in a presidency that
has otherwise been the opposite of libertarian:

00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:00.289
trade wars, travel bans, and big-spending
increases.

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:04.190
Trump has appointed real reformers to carry
out his deregulatory agenda:

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.420
Betsy DeVos at Education, Rick Perry at Energy,

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.960
Scott Gottlieb at the Food and Drug Administration,
Ajit Pai at the FCC.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.200
One of the most influential administrators
is Neomi Rao,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.060
Trump’s appointee to oversee cost-benefit
analyses for the entire regulatory juggernaut,

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.920
who came to the White House after founding
the Center for the Study of the Administrative State

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.600
at George Mason University.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.040
I think that there are a lot of very strongly
reform-minded people

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.650
who have come into the administration

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:34.740
and occupying a number of the senior political
appointments.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:37.861
And they're very committed to regulatory reform

00:01:37.861 --> 00:01:44.220
and rolling back some of these burdens that
had been imposed over the past 5, 10, 15 years.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.800
So what has Trump deregulated?

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:51.080
In his first year the president signed 15
regulatory repeals via the Congressional Review Act,

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.400
or 14 more times than the law had been used
the previous two decades.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.460
Gone are Obamacare’s Individual Mandate,
the EPA’s Clean Power Plan,

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.500
and the Department of Education’s kangaroo
court-style guidance on campus sexual assault.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.620
In addition to the two-for-one executive order,

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:12.700
Trump has also required agencies to impose
an aggregate regulatory cost each year of zero.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:19.440
The never-ending growth of red tape in America
has come to a sudden screeching and beautiful halt.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.860
We’re not just going to eliminate some small,
insignificant rules,

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.960
but we also have to have a negative regulatory
cost budget.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.960
Which is what we had for 2017, and we went
even below that.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.200
And I think it's the first time, since at
least we have been keeping records,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.620
that there has been a year like that.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.540
You know we're also encouraging the agencies
to look at deregulation

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.080
not just from the numbers side.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.900
Many agencies are peeling back guidance documents
or streamlining paperwork burdens,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.400
and all of that I think is having an impact.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.870
The free-market enthusiasts at the Competitive
Enterprise Institute,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.720
a Washington, D.C. think tank,

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.900
have been tallying new regulations annually
for the past 25 years.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.740
In 2017, they found the administration cut
the number of pages in the Federal Register

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.440
by more than a third.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.180
And the 3,281 new rules amounted to:

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:15.640
“the lowest count since records began being
kept in the mid-1970s.”

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:20.780
I haven't seen personally anything like the
regulatory reductions that have taken place.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.940
The Competitive Enterprise Institute’s Clyde
Wayne Crews

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.100
is already calling Donald Trump “the least
regulatory president of all.”

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.580
Trump has gone after, say for example, the
Waters of the United States rule.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.240
The EPA’s so-called Waters of the United
States rule

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.460
is one of the worst examples of federal regulation.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.720
It's welcoming to see the FCC turn back the
so-called net neutrality rule.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:46.120
I'm impressed and unimpressed at the same
time.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.280
I love what has happened, and I want it to
continue.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.520
But to really address all that you’ve got
to get Congress to act.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.540
In other words, the executive branch can rearrange
the scope and process of enforcement,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.800
but only legislators can repeal the underlying
laws.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.440
The million-dollar question is,

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.660
how much impact will the Trump administration’s regulatory tweaks have

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.540
on individual industries and the overall economy?

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.240
So by our very kind of conservative internal
estimates,

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.900
we estimate that the regulations that we've
pulled back are saving more than $8 billion dollars.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.819
Maybe that sounds small,

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.140
but when you think about it in the context
that the cost of regulations increases every year,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.300
I think it's actually pretty impressive.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.480
I think another number that is important,
is that we are revisiting, or have postponed,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:35.580
over 1,500 rules that were on the agenda.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.440
Some of those rules may end up going forward,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:41.820
but I think it demonstrates the magnitude
of what we're reconsidering.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:49.720
Is this the first real challenge to the kind
of automatic growth of the regulatory state

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:52.360
that we've seen in a solid generation here?

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.480
I think so. Yes.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:58.060
We have done more in our first year than any
president since we've been keeping records.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.660
The numbers that had been reduced in the Trump
administration are pretty significant.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:07.300
When he rolled back, or withdrew, or withheld
1,500 of Obama's regulations,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.740
they were not all significant regulations
by any stretch.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.000
But it's a start.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.480
And remember, Obama said, "I have a pen and
phone, and if Congress doesn't act,

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:19.200
we can go around Congress and act."

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.980
Trump has a pen and phone, too.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.140
Any president does.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:27.800
And it can be used within the rule of law
to advance liberty rather than curtail it.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.800
Though Trump met his target last year, can
he do it again?

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.360
Or has the easy stuff already been plucked?

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.720
I think in 2018, he's gonna have a much tougher
time.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.300
The job of rolling back two for one, when
you're acting alone as president

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.360
and you can't make law on your own,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.400
the barrier that you run into is you run out of low-hanging fruit.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.620
So how much can you do now?

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:52.260
You've got two-for-one in place, I know you
want to try to do three-for-one next year,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.460
but how much latitude does the executive branch
have?

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:01.440
There's plenty of bad regulation that's still
out there that we can work on rolling back.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:05.120
To really get at, sort of, the core of the
regulatory state,

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.400
you're going to need statutory reform.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.720
The House has passed a series of reform bills

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.140
that focus on the process by which the executive
branch makes its rules.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.540
These have names like:

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.460
Searching for and Cutting Regulations that
are Unnecessarily Burdensome Act, or “SCRUB,”

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.160
the Regulations from the Executive in Need
of Scrutiny Act, or “REINS, and so on.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.560
All are currently languishing in the Senate,
where the slim Republican majority

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:33.139
includes senators who’ve expressed concern
that deconstructing the administrative state

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:36.560
will have a negative impact on the health
and safety of Americans.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.420
Now, your progressive friends will say that
what you're doing

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:41.880
is making the world more dangerous.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:44.080
What's your basic response to that framework?

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.340
I think Congress has, through a massive mass
of statutes,

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.800
ensured essentially that we live in a very
highly regulated society.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.200
There's a limit to how much the executive
branch can do to undo that,

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.120
and we're not looking to undo health and safety
protections that are required by law.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.220
By definition the regulations that we're turning
back are not working for the public.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:09.199
So can libertarians expect more progress in
the midterm election year of 2018?

00:07:09.199 --> 00:07:12.669
To the extent regulatory reforms happen in
a year like this one,

00:07:12.669 --> 00:07:16.760
that's going to be so contentious and already
is, I think it would be something lesser.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.580
It would be something on the order of more
disclosure of regulations.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:23.360
I know there's some bipartisan support for
that.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:29.460
But sadly, in terms of rolling back regulations
this year, Trump is pretty much on his own.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:30.460
What's in front of you?

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:34.140
We want to reduce the cumulative regulatory
burdens on the American people

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:35.500
as much as possible.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.620
The agencies now really have to go back

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.780
andthink about which of their regulations are no longer working.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:42.620
It's a good government policy.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:45.460
Who is in favor of regulations that aren't
working?

